{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 8, "high": 24, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 4, "high": 30, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.80
    ,
    "why": "Smallest family in the registry: one shared datapath, N+3 cycles/result. It already holds 15/16 of the merged front with best feasible luts_plus_ffs 289, so it is the family that actually defines the area-accuracy trade-off here. Throughput is a non-issue: Fmax/(N+3) is far above the 1 MSPS floor for any N<=30, so the whole budget can go to area/accuracy. Ranges span the HV-relevant band: W 8..24 lets accuracy_bits reach ~22 while keeping area under the 1500 ref for most (W,N) pairs, and N 4..30 covers the accuracy floor (2^-10 needs roughly N>=10-12) up through the high-accuracy corner. Guards and rounding are left free because they trade a little area for accuracy and the model must decide that, not me."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 8, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 4, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.20
    ,
    "why": "Structurally dominated for this spec: k chained micro-rotations/cycle means roughly k copies of the iterative datapath, so area grows with k while the only thing it buys is throughput, which is not an objective (the 1 MSPS floor is already met by iterative). Fleet evidence agrees: 0/16 on the merged front, best area 391 vs 289 for iterative. Keep a small share purely to confirm the dominance and to catch any surprise where a small-k, small-W/N point lands on the front; ranges are capped tighter (W,N<=20) because anything larger is guaranteed to sit above the 1500 area ref."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is min-area with a modest accuracy floor (max_abs_err <= 2^-10) and a trivial throughput floor (>= 1 MSPS). That makes it an area/accuracy problem, not a throughput problem, so the budget should go where the (luts_plus_ffs, accuracy_bits) trade-off lives. Iterative is the smallest family and already owns 15/16 of the merged front (best area 289), so it gets 80% of the budget over a wide W/N range that spans from the accuracy floor up to the 1500-area reference point. unrolled_k gets 20% only as a dominance check, since k chained rotations multiply area and buy throughput that the spec does not reward. The reserved end-of-run budget should map iterative's full ranges, as it is the family found on the front."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Final round and the front is feasible and converged. Both spec constraints are met by designs on the front: throughput_msps >= 1 shows 0% violations (best seen 25.5 MSPS; every front point >= 4.79 MSPS), and max_abs_err <= 0.000976562 is satisfied by the whole front (worst front point 2^-10.12 = 0.000899), so `infeasible` is ruled out. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) lands on iterative [data_width=15, n_iter=23, angle_guard=2, frac_guard=1, rounding=trunc] at luts_plus_ffs=278 with accuracy_bits=10.1 (max_abs_err 0.000899) and 6.23 MSPS — the cheapest feasible point and still above the 10-bit accuracy HV reference. Hypervolume stayed at 8115 with +0.0% gain this round over 113 of 188 evals (62 unique feasible designs), and round 2 explored both remaining families (unrolled_k best feasible area 423, well above iterative's 278; best accuracy 17.08 bits), indicating no further improvement to chase within the budget."
}
